# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiled binaries
#
# `go build` names its output after the directory it builds, so Go binaries
# have no file extension. There is no safe wildcard for that: a bare pattern
# like `server` would also match any directory called `server`. So each
# `package main` directory in this repo gets an anchored line of its own.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/calculator/calculator
/go_with_tests/helloworld/helloworld
/http/htmldownload/htmldownload
/http/htmlparalleldownload/htmlparalleldownload
/http/server/server
/tcpupperecho/tcpupperecho
/urlfetcher/urlfetcher
/userdata/userdata
/writetcp/writetcp

# A better habit for anything new: send the output to bin/ instead of leaving
# it beside the source, and the next line covers you without further edits.
#
#     go build -o bin/thing ./thing
#
/bin/
/dist/

# Cross-compiled and cgo output, which at least has extensions to match on.
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test and profiling output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `go test -c` compiles a test binary rather than running it.
*.test

# Coverage and pprof profiles: go test -coverprofile=coverage.out
*.out

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor and OS droppings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# macOS Finder
.DS_Store

# GoLand / IntelliJ
.idea/

# VS Code
.vscode/

# Vim
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Go workspace
#
# go.work stitches several local modules together and points at paths on one
# machine, so it is normally kept local rather than shared. Delete these two
# lines if you ever decide to commit a workspace on purpose.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
go.work
go.work.sum

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately NOT ignored:
#
#   go.mod, go.sum   Source. They pin the exact dependency versions and
#                    checksums a clone needs to build what you built.
#   vendor/          If you ever run `go mod vendor`, the convention is to
#                    commit the result, not hide it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
